course: dictionary
lesson: 7030
title: Fair and unfair support
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Partiality means unfair support for one person or side over another. Its clue is in the words around it. «Do not pervert justice or show partiality» is an old warning: a judge must treat everyone the same and must not take sides.
explain: «The referee showed partiality.» Read the sentence for clues. A referee should be fair, so showing partiality means favouring one team without being fair. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The referee showed unfair support for one team" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Partiality can also mean a special liking, as in «a partiality for chocolate». The clue is the word for: you have a partiality for something when you really like it. Read the whole sentence before you decide which meaning fits.
fact: Partiality is a noun, and its plural is partialities.
fact: «Partiality» means unfair support for one person or side over another.
fact: «Partiality» can also mean a special liking for something, such as chocolate.
fact: Someone who does not take sides is impartial, which means they do not favour anyone.
fact: A judge who has a friend taking part in a case should step back, which is called a conflict of interest.
exercise: «The referee showed partiality.» What does partiality mean here? | *Unfair support for one side over another | Fair play for every player | Being very tired after a match | A rule about how the ball is kicked
exercise: Ella says, «I have a partiality for apples.» What does she mean? | She is unfair to the apple tree | She is angry with every apple | *She really likes apples | She has never tasted an apple
exercise: Which word means not favouring anyone? | *impartial | partiality | fairytale | partner
test: What is the plural of partiality? | partialitys | partiality's | *partialities | partiales
test: Why should a judge step back when a friend is taking part in a case? | Because judges must always be tired | To show a partiality for the friend | To please the friend | *To avoid a conflict of interest and show no partiality
exam: Which sentence shows unfair partiality? | The judge had a bowl of soup for lunch | *The judge gave the prize to her own nephew, even though he was not the best | The judge walked home in the rain | The judge gave the prize to the best poem in the competition
